## 5. Types of Artificial Intelligence Based on Capabilities

AI is commonly classified into three theoretical categories based on its capabilities.

### 5.1 Artificial Narrow Intelligence (ANI)

Also called Weak AI.

It is designed to perform specific tasks or a limited range of tasks.

Examples:
- Face recognition systems.
- Google Maps route recommendations.
- Spam detection.
- Voice assistants.
- Product recommendation systems.

**Characteristics:**
- Specialized in particular tasks.
- Cannot automatically perform every intellectual task.
- Usually operates within predefined capabilities.
- Most AI applications used today belong to this category.

### 5.2 Artificial General Intelligence (AGI)

Also called Strong AI in some discussions.

AGI refers to a hypothetical AI system capable of learning, understanding and performing a broad range of intellectual tasks at a level comparable to humans.

**Expected Characteristics:**
- Broad problem-solving abilities.
- Ability to learn across different domains.
- Flexible adaptation to unfamiliar tasks.
- Generalized reasoning and knowledge application.

AGI remains a research and theoretical concept. There is no universally accepted demonstration that it has been achieved.

### 5.3 Artificial Super Intelligence (ASI)

ASI refers to a hypothetical AI system that would surpass human intellectual capabilities across most domains.

**Expected Characteristics:**
- Extremely advanced problem-solving.
- Superior analytical capabilities.
- Broad knowledge and learning abilities.
- Potentially advanced scientific discovery.

ASI is speculative and does not currently exist as an established technology.

### Comparison of AI Types

| Feature | ANI | AGI | ASI |
|---|---|---|---|
| Meaning | Narrow AI | General AI | Super AI |
| Capabilities | Specific tasks | Broad human-level abilities | Beyond human-level abilities |
| Existence | Exists today | Hypothetical | Hypothetical |
| Flexibility | Limited | Broad | Extremely broad |
| Example | Spam filter | Hypothetical general AI | Hypothetical superintelligence |

