## 6. Types of AI Based on Functionality

Another theoretical classification divides AI into four categories.

### 6.1 Reactive Machines

These AI systems respond to current inputs without using stored past experiences as memory.

Characteristics:
- No persistent memory of previous interactions.
- Responds to the current situation.
- Performs specific tasks.

Example: IBM Deep Blue, the chess-playing system.

### 6.2 Limited Memory AI

These systems use information from past observations or stored data to make decisions.

Examples:
- Self-driving vehicle perception and decision systems.
- Recommendation systems using historical user activity.
- AI systems using recent conversational context.

Most practical modern AI systems use some form of stored or contextual information.

### 6.3 Theory of Mind AI

This is a theoretical category of AI that would understand aspects of human emotions, beliefs, intentions and social behaviour.

It is an area of research, not an established general AI capability.

### 6.4 Self-Aware AI

This hypothetical category refers to AI possessing self-awareness or consciousness.

There is no established evidence of self-aware AI systems.

### Comparison

| Type | Main Feature | Status |
|---|---|---|
| Reactive Machines | Responds to current inputs | Historical and practical systems |
| Limited Memory | Uses stored or past information | Common today |
| Theory of Mind | Models human mental states | Research / hypothetical |
| Self-Aware | Hypothetical consciousness | Speculative |

